PG.notes("s03-lists", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the Intermediate HTML section with the two list types: &lt;ul&gt; (bullets, order irrelevant) and &lt;ol&gt; (automatic numbers, order matters), both built from &lt;li&gt; items. The exercise turns a plain-text cinnamon roll recipe into a page with headings, two unordered ingredient lists and one ordered list of steps.</p>\n<h3>Why lists matter</h3>\n<ul><li>Lists are one of the most common structures on real websites: 'top 10' style articles, product grids and even photo galleries are often built as lists.</li><li>Example shown: a 'ten most wanted' page where right-click on a photo then Inspect (browser DevTools) reveals that each photo card is an &lt;li&gt; sitting inside a &lt;ul&gt;. So a list does not have to look like bullet points; CSS can turn it into a grid of cards (covered later).</li><li>Habit to build: when you wonder how a site is built, right-click &gt; Inspect and read the element tree.</li></ul>\n<h3>Unordered list: &lt;ul&gt; with &lt;li&gt; children</h3>\n<ul><li>&lt;ul&gt; opens an unordered list and &lt;/ul&gt; closes it. Unlike h1 or p, you do not put text straight between these tags.</li><li>Between the opening and closing ul tags go one or more list item elements: &lt;li&gt;content&lt;/li&gt;. Each li holds exactly one item's content.</li><li>Because a ul contains other elements rather than plain text, its opening and closing tags normally sit on separate lines with the li elements in between.</li><li>A list needs li elements to display anything as a list; the li is what produces each bullet line.</li><li>Default rendering: each li gets a bullet marker (a filled disc) and the whole list is indented from the left.</li><li>Use a ul when the order of items carries no meaning, e.g. a shopping list or a set of ingredients.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ordered list: &lt;ol&gt; with &lt;li&gt; children</h3>\n<ul><li>Identical structure, but the container is &lt;ol&gt;...&lt;/ol&gt;. The li elements inside are written exactly the same way.</li><li>The browser numbers the items automatically, starting at 1. You never type the numbers yourself.</li><li>The number an item gets depends only on its position in the source: move the third li to the top and it becomes number 1, and everything else renumbers.</li><li>Use an ol whenever sequence matters: instructions, step-by-step guides, rankings. Recipe steps are the classic case because you cannot bake before mixing.</li><li>Summary of the difference: ol gives numbers, ul gives bullets; the li items inside are the same in both.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Only li elements should be direct children of ul or ol. Headings, paragraphs or other lists go inside an li, not between them.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Useful ol attributes not shown in this lecture: start=&quot;5&quot; begins counting at 5, reversed counts down, type=&quot;a&quot; / &quot;A&quot; / &quot;i&quot; / &quot;I&quot; switches to letters or Roman numerals. An li can also take value=&quot;10&quot; to jump the count.</li></ul>\n<h3>Writing li elements on one line or several</h3>\n<ul><li>When long text is pasted between an opening and closing tag, VS Code's formatter may move the closing tag (and the content) onto their own lines, so the content sits between two tag lines.</li><li>Both layouts are valid and render identically, because the browser ignores line breaks and extra spaces between tags.</li><li>It is a personal style choice. The instructor prefers each &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt; on a single line, which makes it easy to count items at a glance and keeps the list compact.</li><li>The next lecture covers nesting and indentation in detail.</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise workflow tips</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the '3.1 List Elements' zip from the lecture's Resources, extract it, put the folder in your Web Development Projects folder and open it in VS Code.</li><li>index.html already contains all the recipe text as plain lines; the job is only to add tags. goal.png shows the target, solution.html the answer.</li><li>Open Live Preview next to the editor (right-click index.html &gt; Show Preview) to see each change as you save.</li><li>Fast technique for pre-typed text: create an empty &lt;li&gt;&lt;/li&gt; pair, paste several copies, then drag each line of text (or cut and paste it) between a pair of tags. When writing a page from scratch you would simply type the content inside each li.</li><li>Heading levels are your choice in this exercise; the suggested outline is one h1, h2 for 'Ingredients' and 'Instructions', h3 for the two ingredient sub-headings. The lists are the part that matters.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li>The reference solution uses h3 for 'Instructions' while the lecture suggests h2; either is acceptable here, but h2 keeps it level with 'Ingredients'.</li><li>The reference adds a personal footer line (stripped below) and has a typo ('minuites') in step 4.</li><li>The reference keeps long steps split over several lines inside their li, which shows the formatter behaviour described above.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Unordered list: bullets, order does not matter</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Milk&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Eggs&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Flour&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Ordered list: the browser adds 1, 2, 3 based on source order</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Mix the ingredients&lt;/li&gt;   &lt;!-- shows as 1. --&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Knead the dough&lt;/li&gt;       &lt;!-- shows as 2. --&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Bake&lt;/li&gt;                  &lt;!-- shows as 3. --&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Same li, two formatting styles (identical output)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- one line (instructor's preference) --&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;Cut the roll into 3cm sections and place flat into a baking tray.&lt;/li&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- content on its own line, as the formatter sometimes produces --&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;\n  Cut the roll into 3cm sections and place flat into a baking tray.\n&lt;/li&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> ol attributes</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ol start=&quot;3&quot;&gt;        &lt;!-- 3, 4, 5 --&gt;\n&lt;ol reversed&gt;         &lt;!-- counts down --&gt;\n&lt;ol type=&quot;i&quot;&gt;         &lt;!-- i, ii, iii --&gt;\n&lt;li value=&quot;10&quot;&gt;...&lt;/li&gt; &lt;!-- this item is 10, the next 11 --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.1 List Elements: cinnamon roll recipe</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html holds the recipe title, the labels Ingredients / For the dough: / For the filling: / Instructions, six dough ingredients, three filling ingredients and ten instruction steps, all as untagged text, so the preview is one long run-on line.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Turn the text into a structured recipe page matching goal.png: headings, a bulleted list of dough ingredients, a bulleted list of filling ingredients and a numbered list of the ten steps.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Wrap the recipe title in an h1.</li><li>Wrap 'Ingredients' and 'Instructions' in h2 elements.</li><li>Wrap 'For the dough:' and 'For the filling:' in h3 elements.</li><li>Below the dough heading add &lt;ul&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;; inside it create six &lt;li&gt;&lt;/li&gt; pairs and move one dough ingredient into each.</li><li>Below the filling heading add a second ul containing three li elements, one per filling ingredient.</li><li>Below 'Instructions' add an &lt;ol&gt;&lt;/ol&gt; (the steps must happen in sequence) and put each of the ten steps in its own li.</li><li>Do not type any numbers; let the ol generate them.</li><li>Save, compare the Live Preview with goal.png, then open solution.html to check.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A readable recipe page: title, two headings, two indented bullet lists (6 and 3 items) and an indented list numbered 1 to 10.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Wesley's Recipe for the Best Cinnamon Rolls&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Ingredients&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;For the dough:&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;¾ cup warm milk&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;2 ¼ teaspoons yeast&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;¼ cup granulated sugar&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;1 egg plus 1 egg yolk&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;¼ cup butter&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;3 cups bread flour&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;For the filling:&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;2/3 cup dark brown sugar&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;1 ½ tablespoons ground cinnamon&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;¼ cup butter&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Instructions&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;ol&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Mix the milk with the yeast, sugar, eggs.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Melt the butter and add to the mixture.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Add in the flour and mix until combined into a dough.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Knead the dough for 10 minutes.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Transfer the dough into a large bowl and cover with plastic wrap. Leave it somewhere to rise for 2 hours.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;After the dough has doubled in size, roll it out into a large rectangle.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Melt the butter for the filling and mix in the sugar and cinnamon.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Spread the filling onto the dough then roll the dough into a swiss roll.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Cut the roll into 3cm sections and place flat into a baking tray.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Preheat the oven to 350F or 180C, then bake the rolls for 20-25min until lightly brown.&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ol&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting text directly inside &lt;ul&gt; or &lt;ol&gt; without wrapping each item in &lt;li&gt;.</li><li>Typing '1.' '2.' into ol items, which gives double numbering.</li><li>Choosing ul or ol for the look rather than the meaning (ingredients are unordered, steps are ordered).</li><li>Forgetting a closing &lt;/li&gt; or closing the list before the last item.</li><li>Opening the still-zipped download instead of the extracted folder.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s03-nesting", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to nest a complete list inside a list item, why the parent li's closing tag must come after the nested list, and why consistent indentation is what makes nested HTML readable. The exercise builds a four-level nested list, and the walkthrough ends with VS Code auto-indentation and using indentation to find a missing closing tag.</p>\n<h3>Nesting: elements inside elements</h3>\n<ul><li>Nesting means placing one element inside another. You have already done it: every li sits inside a ul or ol, and the code shows that by indenting the li lines one level further than the list tags.</li><li>A list can also be nested inside another list. The inner ul/ol goes inside one of the outer list's li elements, never directly between li elements.</li><li>Rendered result: the inner list appears indented under the bullet (or number) of the li that contains it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Browsers change the default marker for deeper unordered levels: disc, then circle, then square.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to write a nested list</h3>\n<ul><li>Find the li that should own the sub-list.</li><li>Write that li's own text first (e.g. B), press Enter, then write a complete new &lt;ul&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt; or &lt;ol&gt;...&lt;/ol&gt; with its own li items.</li><li>Only after the nested list's closing tag do you close the parent li. In a simple item the &lt;/li&gt; comes straight after the text; in an item that contains a sub-list the &lt;/li&gt; moves down to after the sub-list.</li><li>This is the key rule of the lesson: the closing tag of the outer element must come after everything nested inside it. Tags close in reverse order of opening.</li><li>You can repeat this to any depth, mixing ul and ol freely: an ordered list inside an unordered one, then an unordered one inside that, and so on.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why indentation matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Indentation (leading spaces or tabs) has no effect on what the browser renders. It exists purely for the people reading the code.</li><li>With every nested level indented one step further, you can see at a glance which list belongs to which item. If everything is flush left, you have to read each tag name to work out the structure.</li><li>The more levels there are, the worse unindented code gets. The lecture shows the same three-list example (an outer ul, a ul under item B, and an ol under the second item of that ul) with and without indentation to make the point.</li><li>Write code with other readers in mind: teammates, reviewers, and you in a few months.</li></ul>\n<h3>VS Code helps: auto-indent and indent guides</h3>\n<ul><li>Saving with Cmd+S (Mac) or Ctrl+S (Windows) re-indents the file automatically, because the formatter knows which elements belong inside which (e.g. li inside ul/ol). This works if format-on-save was enabled in the setup lessons.</li><li>So you do not have to indent perfectly by hand while typing, but you do need to understand what the indentation means.</li><li>VS Code draws thin vertical guide lines linking an opening tag to its matching closing tag; the children sit indented between them.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> You can also run Format Document on demand: Shift+Option+F on Mac, Shift+Alt+F on Windows.</li></ul>\n<h3>Using indentation to debug a missing closing tag</h3>\n<ul><li>Most HTML elements come in pairs, so every opening tag should have a matching closing tag at the same indentation level.</li><li>Example from the walkthrough: delete the &lt;/ol&gt; that should close the B1-B3 list. In the preview, item C suddenly shows up numbered as part of the ordered list instead of as a top-level bullet.</li><li>To diagnose it, follow the guide lines: the outer ul matches its closing tag, each li matches its closing tag, but the ol lines up with a closing &lt;/li&gt; instead of a closing &lt;/ol&gt;. That mismatch points straight at the missing tag.</li><li>Finding and fixing mistakes like this is called debugging, which gets much more attention later in the course. Indentation is the first debugging tool.</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise workflow tips</h3>\n<ul><li>Download and extract the 'Nesting and Indentation' zip from the lecture Resources and open it in VS Code.</li><li>Keep goal.png open next to the Live Preview and compare after each step.</li><li>Build from the outside in: first the outer list, then the first sub-list, then the next level down, checking the preview each time.</li><li>The item names encode their position (B2a = item a of the list under B2), which tells you where each list belongs.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Vocabulary that the rest of the course relies on</h3>\n<ul><li>An element directly inside another is its child; the wrapping element is its parent; elements with the same parent are siblings; anything inside at any depth is a descendant.</li><li>In the exercise, the ol (B1-B3) is a child of the li 'B' and a descendant of the outer ul.</li><li>These relationships drive CSS selectors and JavaScript DOM navigation later.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">A list nested inside a list item: note where the parent &lt;/li&gt; goes</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;A&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;\n    B\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;B1&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;B2&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/li&gt;          &lt;!-- closes 'B' only after the nested list --&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;C&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The same code without indentation: valid, renders the same, much harder to read</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ul&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;A&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;\nB\n&lt;ul&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;B1&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;B2&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;C&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Debugging example: the missing &lt;/ol&gt; makes C render as item 4 of the ordered list</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;A&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;\n    B\n    &lt;ol&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;B1&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;B2&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;B3&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;!-- &lt;/ol&gt; is missing here --&gt;\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;C&lt;/li&gt;   &lt;!-- ends up treated as part of the ol --&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.2 Nesting and Indentation: four-level nested list</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html is the page to write the list into (the reference repo copy is already completed); goal.png shows the target nested list with items named A, B, C, B1, B2, B3, B2a, B2b, B2c, B2aa, B2ab, B31, B32.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Recreate the goal's nested list structure exactly, using the right list type (ul for bullets, ol for numbers) at each level and correct nesting.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create the top-level ul with three li items: A, B, C. Check the preview.</li><li>Inside li 'B', after the text B, add an ol with three items: B1, B2, B3. Move B's closing &lt;/li&gt; to after this ol.</li><li>Inside li 'B2', after its text, add a ul with three items: B2a, B2b, B2c. Close B2's li after that ul.</li><li>Inside li 'B2a' (the first item, not the second), after its text, add a ul with B2aa and B2ab.</li><li>Inside li 'B3', after its text, add an ol with B31 and B32.</li><li>Save (Cmd/Ctrl+S) so VS Code re-indents everything, then compare the preview with goal.png level by level.</li><li>If something lands at the wrong level, follow the indent guides to find the opening tag whose closing partner is missing or misplaced.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Bullets A, B, C at the top; under B a list numbered 1-3; under B2 three bullets; under B2a two further bullets; under B3 a list numbered 1-2. Preview identical to goal.png.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;A&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;\n      B\n      &lt;ol&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;B1&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;\n          B2\n          &lt;ul&gt;\n            &lt;li&gt;\n              B2a\n              &lt;ul&gt;\n                &lt;li&gt;B2aa&lt;/li&gt;\n                &lt;li&gt;B2ab&lt;/li&gt;\n              &lt;/ul&gt;\n            &lt;/li&gt;\n            &lt;li&gt;B2b&lt;/li&gt;\n            &lt;li&gt;B2c&lt;/li&gt;\n          &lt;/ul&gt;\n        &lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;\n          B3\n          &lt;ol&gt;\n            &lt;li&gt;B31&lt;/li&gt;\n            &lt;li&gt;B32&lt;/li&gt;\n          &lt;/ol&gt;\n        &lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ol&gt;\n    &lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;C&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Closing the parent &lt;/li&gt; straight after its text and then putting the sub-list after it. The sub-list then sits directly inside the outer ul/ol, which is invalid, and the structure no longer matches the goal.</li><li>Putting a nested list between two li elements instead of inside one.</li><li>Forgetting a closing &lt;/ul&gt; or &lt;/ol&gt;, which pulls later items (like C) into the wrong list.</li><li>Nesting B2aa/B2ab under B2b instead of B2a.</li><li>Using ul where the goal shows numbers (or ol where it shows bullets).</li><li>Assuming indentation changes the output. It does not; it only reveals the structure.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s03-anchors", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces HTML attributes through the anchor element: &lt;a&gt; only becomes a working hyperlink once it has an href attribute holding the destination URL. Covers attribute syntax, element-specific versus global attributes (draggable as the example), and an exercise that builds an ordered list of five favourite-website links, with a bonus step using the ol start attribute.</p>\n<h3>What an attribute is and where it goes</h3>\n<ul><li>An attribute adds extra information to an element, for example where a link should point.</li><li>Attributes always go inside the opening tag: after the tag name, before the closing angle bracket &gt;. Never in the closing tag.</li><li>Syntax: tag name, a space, the attribute name, =, then the value in double quotes. Example: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.producthunt.com/&quot;&gt;.</li><li>An element can have any number of attributes. Separate them with spaces, all inside the same opening tag: &lt;a href=&quot;...&quot; draggable=&quot;true&quot;&gt;.</li><li>Why the quotes: the value is plain text (a string), and the quotes separate that text from HTML's own reserved words such as a, li or href. A URL is just text, so it is quoted.</li><li>VS Code autocompletion helps: start typing href inside the opening tag, pick it from the suggestions and press Enter. It inserts href=&quot;&quot; and places the cursor between the quotes ready for the URL.</li></ul>\n<h3>The anchor element &lt;a&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>&lt;a&gt; and &lt;/a&gt; wrap the clickable content of a hyperlink. The text between the tags is what the user sees and clicks.</li><li>Without an href attribute the element is not an active link: the text displays as ordinary text and nothing happens on click.</li><li>Add href=&quot;URL&quot; and the browser immediately styles it as a link (blue and underlined by default) and clicking it navigates to that URL.</li><li>href is the anchor's key attribute. MDN's page for &lt;a&gt; lists all of its element-specific attributes; only a few are used in the course, and some listed there are deprecated and no longer used in HTML5.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Other common anchor attributes: target=&quot;_blank&quot; opens the link in a new tab; title=&quot;...&quot; shows a tooltip on hover; download suggests saving the linked file. href can also be a relative path (another page of your own site, covered in the next section), #id (jump within the page) or mailto:address.</li></ul>\n<h3>Element-specific vs global attributes</h3>\n<ul><li>Element-specific attributes only make sense on certain elements: href on &lt;a&gt;, start on &lt;ol&gt;, and so on.</li><li>Global attributes are available on every HTML element. MDN keeps a separate list of them.</li><li>Example used: draggable takes true or false and sets whether the element can be dragged. With draggable=&quot;true&quot; on the anchor you can click and drag it around; without it, a drag attempt just selects the text.</li><li>Same syntax as any other attribute: name, =, quoted value, inside the opening tag.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Global attributes you will meet constantly: id, class, style, title, lang, hidden. Note that links with an href and images are already draggable by default in most browsers, so draggable shows its effect most clearly on elements like p or div; real drag-and-drop behaviour also needs JavaScript.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nesting links in a list</h3>\n<ul><li>For a list of links the structure is three layers deep: ol &gt; li &gt; a. The anchor sits inside the list item, which sits inside the ordered list.</li><li>The list numbers are not blue because they come from the ol/li, not from the anchor; only the anchor's content is styled as a link.</li><li>Reading MDN for an element (here &lt;ol&gt;) to find extra attributes is a skill the course wants you to practise.</li></ul>\n<h3>The ol start attribute (bonus challenge)</h3>\n<ul><li>MDN's &lt;ol&gt; page lists a start attribute: an integer that sets the number the list counts from.</li><li>Put it in the opening ol tag: &lt;ol start=&quot;5&quot;&gt;. A five-item list then shows 5 to 9 instead of 1 to 5.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Related ol attributes: reversed (counts down) and type (1, a, A, i, I).</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li>The reference solution links to the repo author's own profiles (GitHub, portfolio, LinkedIn, Instagram); those personal links are replaced with neutral examples in the solution below, and the author footer is dropped.</li><li>It writes the list twice, once plain and once with start=&quot;5&quot;, to show both the main task and the bonus. One list is enough; add or remove start as needed.</li><li>Long anchors are wrapped over several lines by the formatter; that has no effect on the output.</li></ul>\n<h3>Study habit</h3>\n<ul><li>After each lesson, jot down anything that was new to you; the course deliberately reuses earlier topics (lists, nesting) to reinforce them.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Attribute anatomy: name=&quot;value&quot; inside the opening tag</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- tag  attribute name  =  value (quoted)          content        closing tag --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.producthunt.com/&quot;&gt;Product Hunt&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">No href = not a link; with href = a working link</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;a&gt;Product Hunt&lt;/a&gt;                                   &lt;!-- plain text, not clickable --&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.producthunt.com/&quot;&gt;Product Hunt&lt;/a&gt; &lt;!-- blue, underlined, clickable --&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Two attributes: one element-specific (href), one global (draggable)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.producthunt.com/&quot; draggable=&quot;true&quot;&gt;Product Hunt&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Bonus: start the numbering at 5</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;ol start=&quot;5&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;  &lt;!-- shows 5. --&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;  &lt;!-- shows 6. --&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.3 Anchor Elements: my top 5 favourite websites</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html contains only the h1 'My Top 5 Favourite Websites'. goal.png shows an ordered list numbered 1-5 where each entry's text is a blue clickable link.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build an ordered list of five links to your own favourite websites, each opening the right site when clicked. Bonus: make the list count from 5 instead of 1.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Under the h1, add an &lt;ol&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;.</li><li>Inside it add five &lt;li&gt;&lt;/li&gt; items.</li><li>Inside each li add an &lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and type the site's name between the tags as the link text.</li><li>In each opening a tag add href=&quot;...&quot; with the full URL (including https://) of that site.</li><li>Open Live Preview and click each link to check it goes to the right place.</li><li>Bonus: look up &lt;ol&gt; on MDN, find the start attribute, and add start=&quot;5&quot; to the opening ol tag so the list reads 5 to 9.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A numbered list of five site names; the names are blue and underlined, the numbers are not, and every link navigates to its site. With the bonus, numbering runs 5-9.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;My Top 5 Favourite Websites&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;ol start=&quot;5&quot;&gt;  &lt;!-- start=&quot;5&quot; is the bonus; remove it for 1-5 --&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.producthunt.com/&quot;&gt;Product Hunt&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/&quot;&gt;MDN Web Docs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.wikipedia.org/&quot;&gt;Wikipedia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://codepen.io/&quot;&gt;CodePen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://stellarium-web.org/&quot;&gt;Stellarium&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving out href, so the text shows but is not a link.</li><li>Putting the attribute in the closing tag or outside the angle brackets.</li><li>Forgetting the quotes around the value, or mixing quote types (href=&quot;...').</li><li>Missing https:// so the browser treats the address as a relative path on your own site.</li><li>Putting the a outside the li (directly inside the ol) instead of inside it.</li><li>Putting start on an li or in the closing &lt;/ol&gt; instead of the opening ol tag.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s03-images", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the &lt;img&gt; element: a void (self-closing) element whose src attribute points to the image file and whose alt attribute gives a text description for screen readers. Also shows picsum.photos for placeholder images, a screen-reader demo of why alt text matters, and an exercise adding a cat or dog image (including an animated GIF) under a heading.</p>\n<h3>The img element and its src attribute</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: &lt;img src=&quot;URL&quot; /&gt;. As with the anchor's href, the attribute does the real work: src (short for source) tells the browser where the image file is.</li><li>The value of src is the image's location, e.g. a full URL to an image hosted online. (Extra) It can also be a path to a file in your own project, which the next section covers.</li><li>img has no closing tag. It is a self-closing tag, also called a void element, like &lt;hr /&gt; and &lt;br /&gt; from the previous section.</li><li>The reason it has no closing tag: there is no text content to wrap. The content is the picture loaded from src.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The trailing slash in &lt;img ... /&gt; is optional in HTML5; &lt;img ...&gt; works the same. The course writes the slash.</li></ul>\n<h3>Placeholder images with picsum.photos</h3>\n<ul><li>picsum.photos serves random photos for use while building a layout, the image equivalent of Lorem Ipsum placeholder text.</li><li>Add the size after a slash: https://picsum.photos/200 returns a random 200 x 200 pixel image. Each load may give a different photo.</li><li>Use it when you need some image in place to see the layout but do not care which one.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> https://picsum.photos/300/200 gives a 300 wide by 200 high image.</li></ul>\n<h3>The alt attribute and accessibility</h3>\n<ul><li>alt stands for alternative text: a short written description of what the image shows. Always include it.</li><li>Blind and visually impaired users browse with a screen reader, which reads page content aloud. When it reaches an image, it reads the alt text, so the description is how they learn what the image is.</li><li>Demo: a Chrome extension (Silktide) that simulates a screen reader on a news site. Stepping through the page, it announces an image by reading its description. Right-click the image &gt; Inspect shows that description is exactly the img's alt value.</li><li>The tool itself is not important (it may only run on some systems); the point is that alt text is what disabled users depend on.</li><li>Adding alt changes nothing visually for sighted users; it works behind the scenes.</li><li>If an image carries no meaning for someone reading the content (purely decorative), the alt can be left empty.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Write empty as alt=&quot;&quot; rather than leaving the attribute out: an empty alt tells screen readers to skip the image, while a missing alt may make them read the file name. alt text is also shown if the image fails to load and is used by search engines.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Good alt text describes the content ('puppy digging in the sand'), not the fact that it is an image ('image of a dog').</li></ul>\n<h3>Image formats</h3>\n<ul><li>GIFs go in src exactly like JPEG or PNG files. An animated GIF starts playing on its own once the page loads; no extra code is needed.</li><li>The exercise offers a static JPEG (kitten) and an animated GIF (puppy).</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise workflow</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the lesson zip, move the extracted folder into the Web Development Projects folder and open it in VS Code.</li><li>index.html provides two image URLs, one cat and one dog. Pick either; the goal is to practise writing an img element.</li><li>Check the result in Live Preview.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li>The reference solution builds both versions, cat then dog, separated by an &lt;hr /&gt;. The lecture only asks for one heading and one image.</li><li>It includes the author's footer line, which is not part of the exercise and is left out below.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Basic image: void element, src gives the location</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;img src=&quot;https://picsum.photos/200&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Always add an alt description</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;img src=&quot;https://picsum.photos/200&quot; alt=&quot;forest at sunset&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Decorative image: empty alt so screen readers skip it</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;img src=&quot;divider-swirl.png&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.4 Image Elements: cat person or dog person</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html contains two example image URLs: a kitten JPEG and an animated puppy GIF, both hosted on the course's GitHub (raw.githubusercontent.com/appbrewery/webdev/main/). goal1.png and goal2.png show the cat and dog versions of the finished page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Add an h1 declaring whether you are a cat person or a dog person, followed immediately by an img showing the matching animal, with a descriptive alt.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add an h1 with the text 'I am a Cat Person' or 'I am a Dog Person'.</li><li>Right after it, add an img element as a self-closing tag (no closing &lt;/img&gt;).</li><li>Set src to the matching URL from index.html (kitten.jpeg or puppy.gif).</li><li>Open Live Preview: the cat is a still photo, the puppy GIF animates by itself.</li><li>Add an alt attribute describing the image, e.g. 'puppy digging in the sand' or 'kitten held in hand'. The preview looks the same; the benefit is for screen-reader users.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A heading with the chosen animal's picture below it; the dog version shows an animated GIF. Inspecting the img shows both src and alt.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;I am a Dog Person&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;img\n    src=&quot;https://raw.githubusercontent.com/appbrewery/webdev/main/puppy.gif&quot;\n    alt=&quot;puppy digging in the sand&quot;\n  /&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- or, for cat people: --&gt;\n  &lt;!--\n  &lt;h1&gt;I am a Cat Person&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;img\n    src=&quot;https://raw.githubusercontent.com/appbrewery/webdev/main/kitten.jpeg&quot;\n    alt=&quot;kitten held in hand&quot;\n  /&gt;\n  --&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing a closing &lt;/img&gt; tag. img is a void element.</li><li>Leaving out alt, which leaves screen-reader users with no description.</li><li>Writing alt text like 'image' or 'photo' that tells the listener nothing.</li><li>Typos in the src URL: the image silently fails and only a broken-image icon (or the alt text) appears.</li><li>Using a page URL (an HTML page that shows the picture) instead of the direct image file URL.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s03-birthday", "<p class=\"summary\">End-of-section project: build an unstyled birthday invitation page that combines headings, an image with alt text, a list and a link. It is an open-ended project rather than a graded exercise, with a minimum of one image, one list and one anchor; the sample solution is walked through briefly.</p>\n<h3>Project vs coding exercise</h3>\n<ul><li>Projects are guidelines, not tests. There is no single correct answer; the aim is practice and developing your own style.</li><li>Minimum requirements: at least one img, at least one list, at least one anchor (a) tag. Everything else (wording, extra headings, extra sections) is up to you.</li><li>The page will look plain, like a 1990s website, because no CSS has been covered yet. Styling comes in later sections.</li><li>If you are happy with the result, take a screenshot and share it in the course Q&amp;A.</li></ul>\n<h3>Starting files</h3>\n<ul><li>Download and extract the lesson zip from the lecture resources.</li><li>index.html supplies an example image URL (a birthday cake photo hosted on the course's GitHub) and an example Google Maps link to use for the location.</li><li>goal.png shows one possible finished page. The Maps link leads to a joke location in Tokyo as an Easter egg.</li></ul>\n<h3>Elements the project pulls together</h3>\n<ul><li>Headings for hierarchy: one h1 for the main message, h2 for the date, h3 for the sub-sections ('What to bring', 'Where to go'). Keep one h1 and do not skip levels.</li><li>img (void element) with src set to the image URL and alt describing the picture for screen-reader users.</li><li>ul with one li per item for the things guests should bring (order does not matter, so unordered).</li><li>a with an href holding the Google Maps URL; the text between the tags (e.g. 'Google map link') is what guests click.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Good additions from earlier lessons: a p of invitation text, an hr between sections, an ol for a timeline of the day, br for a line break in an address.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the sample solution is built</h3>\n<ul><li>h1: the main announcement that it is your birthday.</li><li>h2: the date of the party.</li><li>img: src from the starter code plus an alt description of the cake. If the image appears and has alt text, that part is done.</li><li>h3 'What to bring' followed by a ul, each item in its own li.</li><li>h3 telling guests where to go, followed by an anchor whose href is the supplied Maps link and whose link text says it is a Google Maps link.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li>The reference solution matches the sample described in the lecture (h1, h2, img with alt, h3 + ul, h3 + map link).</li><li>It spells 'Balloons' as 'Baloons' (fixed below) and includes the author's footer line, which is left out.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Skeleton of the required parts: image, list, link</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;It's My Birthday!&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;On the 10th of October&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;IMAGE_URL&quot; alt=&quot;what the picture shows&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;What to bring:&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;Where to go:&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;MAP_URL&quot;&gt;Google map link&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.5 Birthday Invite Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with the boilerplate, an example image URL (birthday-cake3.4.jpeg on raw.githubusercontent.com/appbrewery/webdev/main/) and an example Google Maps link; goal.png shows one possible result.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a party invitation web page that tells guests it is your birthday, when it is, what to bring and where to go. Must contain at least one image, one list and one link.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add an h1 announcing the birthday.</li><li>Add an h2 with the date.</li><li>Add an img with src set to the supplied cake image URL (or your own) and an alt that describes it.</li><li>Add an h3 'What to bring' and a ul below it, one li per item.</li><li>Add an h3 telling guests where to go and an a element below it with href set to the supplied Google Maps link and readable link text.</li><li>Preview, click the link to check it opens the map, and adjust content as you like.</li><li>Optional: screenshot the page and share it in the Q&amp;A.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> An unstyled, browser-default page: large heading, smaller date heading, the cake photo, a bulleted list of items to bring and a blue underlined map link that opens Google Maps.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;It's My Birthday!&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;On the 10th of October&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;img\n    src=&quot;https://raw.githubusercontent.com/appbrewery/webdev/main/birthday-cake3.4.jpeg&quot;\n    alt=&quot;purple birthday cake with candles&quot;\n  /&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;What to bring:&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Balloons (I love balloons)&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Cake (I'm really good at eating)&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;An appetite (there will be lots of food)&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;This is where you need to go:&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;!-- use the Google Maps link supplied in the starter index.html --&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com/maps/@35.7040744,139.5577317,3a,75y,289.6h,87.01t,0.72r/data=!3m6!1e1!3m4!1sgT28ssf0BB2LxZ63JNcL1w!2e0!7i13312!8i6656&quot;&gt;Google map link&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the alt attribute on the image.</li><li>Typing list items without li wrappers inside the ul.</li><li>Pasting the map URL as visible text instead of putting it in the href of an anchor.</li><li>Using several h1 elements for the different sections instead of h2/h3.</li><li>Trying to style the page with attributes; styling waits for CSS.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l023", "<p class=\"summary\">Study-habit tip: code a little every day and mark each day on a calendar so the growing streak keeps you going until coding becomes routine.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Steady daily practice (for example an hour a day over 100 days) beats occasional bursts such as one weekend and then nothing.</li><li>The goal is a habit: once practice is automatic you stop deciding each day whether to do it.</li><li>Calendar method (the 'don't break the chain' idea credited to Jerry Seinfeld): cross off every day you code. The unbroken line becomes its own motivation not to skip a day.</li><li>After roughly 30-40 days the routine tends to stick and needs much less willpower.</li><li>Above all, keep showing up; consistent work produces results.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l024", "<p class=\"summary\">Optional sign-up article for the instructor's monthly email newsletter (the L.A.B. Report). No course content or exercise.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>The newsletter is a once-a-month personal email covering what the instructor has been learning, side projects, and conversations with people in the tech industry about tools and trends.</li><li>Each issue includes three recommended reads picked that month, on subjects like design, programming, productivity and startup growth.</li><li>A sample issue is linked so you can judge it before subscribing.</li><li>Signing up is entirely optional and uses a Udemy-student sign-up link in the article; only join if you actually want the emails.</li></ul>");
